    "output_tail": " least one element.\n\nIt holds 4,072 `[regex, replacement]` pairs (about 0.5 MB), well under both limits. It was generated by `/app/gen.py`, so you can edit and rebuild it.\n\n**Test results:**\n- **`check.py`:** all 18 positions from Morphy's Opera Game pass.\n- **Random positions:** 58 positions from random games all match python-chess.\n- **Special cases:** en passant (including a pinned capture), castling through or into check, castling-rights changes, promotion by push and capture, and a king under attack from a queen right next to it all match.\n\n**How it works:**\n1. **Unpack the board:** the board becomes 64 characters with `.` for empty squares, followed by the castling rights and en-passant fields.\n2. **Make candidate lines:** the position is copied once per square. Copies whose square doesn't hold a white piece are dropped, and each remaining copy is expanded into one line per square that piece's move pattern could reach.\n3. **Apply moves:** a regex for each from-square, target square and piece type checks the move is allowed (clear path, target not a white piece, pawn rules, en passant, promotion to queen) and plays it. Lines where no move applied are deleted.\n4. **Check test:** for each king square, one combined regex deletes the line if a black knight, pawn, king or sliding piece attacks the white king.\n5. **Castling:** when castling, kings are temporarily placed on the squares the king passes through. The check test then catches castling out of, through or into check, after which the real king and rook positions are restored.\n6. **Castling rights:** a right is removed whenever the king or the relevant rook is no longer on its starting square. This also covers capturing a black rook on its home square.\n7. **Repack:** each line is turned back into a FEN with `b` to move and move counters `0 0`. The en-passant square is set on every two-square pawn push; the checker accepts this even when no capture is possible.\n\nEach position takes about 0.7 seconds to process.",
